WEBVTT

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.005
Hello.

00:00:03.215 --> 00:00:04.570
I am Sergey Morozov.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.673
For four and a half years,
the 3R Swiss Imaging Network

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:11.651
has used AI in everyday radiology.

00:00:11.861 --> 00:00:13.845
Here is what we learned along the way.

00:00:14.735 --> 00:00:19.483
When a computer starts thinking slower
than we do, we simply replace it.

00:00:19.693 --> 00:00:25.333
So what should we do with an AI whose answer
arrives after the report is already signed?

00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:30.137
At the 3R Swiss Imaging Network,
we measured exactly that

00:00:30.347 --> 00:00:32.102
in routine clinical work.

00:00:32.312 --> 00:00:36.933
Four and a half years, 20 centers,
10 AI tools from 7 vendors,

00:00:37.143 --> 00:00:41.069
and nearly 400,000 AI-assisted exams.

00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:44.666
Everyone asks whether AI is accurate.

00:00:44.876 --> 00:00:50.043
Far fewer ask whether its answer
arrives in time to make a difference.

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:55.041
To find out, we triangulated
three data sources.

00:00:55.251 --> 00:01:00.037
The AI platform dashboard with
almost 100,000 exams, the RIS

00:01:00.247 --> 00:01:06.465
with about 21,000 exams read with
and without AI, and 58 radiologists

00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:08.182
whom we surveyed twice.

00:01:09.122 --> 00:01:10.762
We looked at three clocks.

00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:12.975
Clock one is AI latency.

00:01:13.185 --> 00:01:16.387
About two minutes from the
end of the scan to the result.

00:01:16.597 --> 00:01:19.190
The algorithm itself needs
only a fraction of that.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.907
Almost three-quarters of
the time is data routing.

00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:25.717
Picture a sprinter stuck in traffic.

00:01:26.607 --> 00:01:29.835
Clock two asks whether the
result arrives in time.

00:01:30.045 --> 00:01:34.238
More than eighty percent of results are ready
before the radiologist starts the report.

00:01:34.448 --> 00:01:37.536
About 7% arrive only after
the report is signed.

00:01:37.746 --> 00:01:40.205
We call this the Too Late rate.

00:01:40.415 --> 00:01:43.789
And to our knowledge, it is the
first time it has been measured.

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:50.356
Clock three is the radiologist's report time.
When the AI result is there in time, it pays off.

00:01:50.566 --> 00:01:56.586
Reports were 26% faster in trauma
radiography and 18% faster in knee MRI,

00:01:56.743 --> 00:01:58.726
adjusted for the individual radiologist.

00:01:58.936 --> 00:02:04.481
Trauma radiography alone is estimated to free
about two-thirds of one radiologist's working year.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:08.603
That is roughly a three-to-one return
on the cost of the AI solution,

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.333
and still about two-to-one
after adjustment.

00:02:12.223 --> 00:02:15.083
On top of the clocks, we asked
the radiologists themselves.

00:02:15.293 --> 00:02:17.390
More than nine in ten use AI,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.555
and they rate their AI tools
above seven out of ten.

00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:24.272
Yet their perceived productivity
stays just below three out of five,

00:02:24.482 --> 00:02:26.066
under the neutral midpoint.

00:02:26.276 --> 00:02:29.459
Economists know this
as the Jevons paradox.

00:02:29.669 --> 00:02:32.395
Time saved may simply
turn into more cases.

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:36.316
From this triangulation
come our monitoring metrics.

00:02:36.526 --> 00:02:39.387
The three clocks tracked
per tool every quarter.

00:02:39.597 --> 00:02:42.742
We also propose a standardized
questionnaire for radiologists

00:02:42.952 --> 00:02:44.307
and two practical steps.

00:02:44.517 --> 00:02:46.976
Push studies to AI as soon
as the scan is complete,

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.745
and put time-to-display
targets into contracts.

00:02:50.635 --> 00:02:52.332
And then comes the real paradox.

00:02:52.542 --> 00:02:55.843
Today, AI sometimes cannot
keep up with the radiologist.

00:02:56.053 --> 00:02:56.970
Fix that,

00:02:57.164 --> 00:03:00.938
and with rising volumes, the radiologist
may no longer keep up with AI.

00:03:01.148 --> 00:03:04.046
Will we still be able
to monitor what it does?

00:03:05.186 --> 00:03:07.112
So we have to do two things.

00:03:07.322 --> 00:03:10.927
Monitor AI and prepare
radiologists to work with it.

00:03:11.137 --> 00:03:12.968
AI should run like a Swiss train.

00:03:13.178 --> 00:03:17.257
On time, comfortable, and taking
us exactly where we need to go.

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:23.942
Right result, right moment, from the 3R
Swiss Imaging Network, published in JACR.

00:03:24.152 --> 00:03:27.494
If you have questions, write
to the 3R research team.
